      <h1>Hanfu Fans: The Essential Elegance Accessory</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
        The fan (shanzi) is more than a cooling tool -- it is the most expressive and versatile Hanfu accessory. A well-chosen fan transforms a static outfit into a dynamic presence. It adds movement to photographs, conveys mood through gesture, and instantly orients the viewer to which dynasty style you are channeling.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>History and Cultural Role</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">
        Chinese fans date back over 3,000 years. In the Han Dynasty, fans were primarily ceremonial -- large, rigid fans held by attendants to shield nobility from the sun. By the Tang Dynasty, the round silk fan (tuanshan) had become a personal accessory for women, often painted with flowers, birds, or calligraphy. The folding fan (zheshan) arrived later, likely via Japan and Korea during the Song Dynasty, and was enthusiastically adopted by the scholar class -- a folded fan tucked into the hand became as much a marker of a gentleman as a sword.
      </p>
      <p class="text-muted">
        In the Hanfu revival, fans have become the most popular accessory after hairpins. They solve the eternal Hanfu photoshoot problem: what do I do with my hands? They add visual interest in still photos through their shape, color, and surface decoration. And they bring a beautiful, quiet interactivity to wearing Hanfu -- the motion of opening and closing a fan, the whisper of silk and bamboo.
      </p>

      <h2>Types of Chinese Fans for Hanfu</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Tuanshan (Round Silk Fan)</h3>
          <p>A flat, round or oval fan with a silk surface stretched over a bamboo or wooden frame, mounted on a long handle. The quintessential Tang Dynasty fan. The silk surface is often painted with flowers, landscapes, or poetry. Also called gongshan (palace fan) when made for the imperial court.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Zheshan (Folding Fan)</h3>
          <p>The iconic folding fan of ribs (usually bamboo or sandalwood) connected by a paper or silk leaf. When closed, it is a compact stick; when opened, a graceful arc. Favored by Song and Ming scholars, who often had them painted with calligraphy by famous artists.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Feather Fan (Yushan)</h3>
          <p>A fan made from goose, crane, or pheasant feathers mounted on a handle. Associated with Daoist immortals and the strategic genius Zhuge Liang of the Three Kingdoms era. Large feather fans create dramatic silhouette effects in photos.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Palm-Leaf Fan (Pukui Shan)</h3>
          <p>A simple, rustic fan made from dried palm leaves or woven bamboo. Historically used by commoners and farmers. In modern Hanfu photography, it adds texture contrast with silk garments and creates a casual, natural look.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>How to Match Fans to Your Hanfu Dynasty Style</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Dynasty Style</th><th>Best Fan Choice</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Han Dynasty</strong></td><td>Large round silk fan, feather fan</td><td>Matches the era's ceremonial, dignified aesthetic</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Tang Dynasty</strong></td><td>Tuanshan (round silk fan), painted with peonies</td><td>The classic Tang accessory; rich colors match Tang opulence</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Song Dynasty</strong></td><td>Folding fan with calligraphy, small round silk fan</td><td>Scholar aesthetic; subtle, intellectual, refined</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Ming Dynasty</strong></td><td>Folding fan with gold-flecked paper, carved sandalwood ribs</td><td>Ming fans were status objects; sandalwood, ivory, and gold accents</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Modern Hanfu</strong></td><td>Simple folding fan, minimalist tuanshan</td><td>Clean, contemporary designs in cotton or plain silk</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Wedding Hanfu</strong></td><td>Red silk tuanshan with gold embroidery</td><td>The round fan shape symbolizes completeness and union</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Where to Buy Hanfu Fans</h2>

      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Online Stores</h3>
        <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
          <li><strong>Etsy:</strong> Best for hand-painted silk fans and vintage-style pieces. Search "Chinese silk fan" or "hanfu fan." Price: $15--$150 USD. Shops like "SilkFanStudio" and "JiangnanArt" offer custom-painted options.</li>
          <li><strong>Amazon:</strong> Good for budget folding fans and basic tuanshan. Quality is variable; read reviews carefully. Price: $8--$40 USD.</li>
          <li><strong>Hanfu Story / NewMoonDance:</strong> Carry fans designed to coordinate with their Hanfu collections. Price: $12--$50 USD.</li>
          <li><strong>Taobao:</strong> Largest selection by far. Search "tuanshan" or "zheshan hanfu." Top shops: Shanlinji, Yipin Shanfang. Price: 20--500 RMB ($3--$70 USD).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Buying in China</h3>
        <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
          <li><strong>Hangzhou -- Wangxingji Fan Shop (Hefang Street):</strong> China's most famous fan brand, established 1875. Handmade sandalwood folding fans from 200--2,000+ RMB. An experience as much as a purchase.</li>
          <li><strong>Suzhou -- Tanmuzhe Fan Shop (Pingjiang Road):</strong> Specializes in hand-painted silk fans by local artists. Price: 100--600 RMB.</li>
          <li><strong>Beijing -- Liulichang Cultural Street:</strong> Multiple traditional craft shops selling calligraphy fans and antique-style pieces.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Price Guide</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Materials</th><th>Price Range (USD)</th><th>What to Expect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Budget</strong></td><td>Printed nylon, cheap bamboo, machine-printed paper</td><td>$5 -- $20</td><td>Good for one-time use or trying a style. Colors may fade.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Mid-Range</strong></td><td>Real bamboo ribs, printed silk, basic hand-painting</td><td>$20 -- $80</td><td>Good daily-use quality. Silk surface with decent print/artwork.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Premium</strong></td><td>Sandalwood ribs, hand-painted silk, gold-leaf paper</td><td>$80 -- $300</td><td>Genuine artisan work. Sandalwood releases fragrance when opened.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Collector</strong></td><td>Aged sandalwood, ivory (antique only), museum-grade painting</td><td>$300 -- $2,000+</td><td>Heirloom quality. Signed by recognized fan artisans.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Styling Tips</h2>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Photos:</strong> Hold the fan slightly away from your body to create depth. A partially opened folding fan near the face draws attention to your eyes. A round silk fan held at waist level creates elegant, classical compositions.</li>
        <li><strong>Color coordination:</strong> Match the fan's dominant color to an accent color in your Hanfu, not the main color -- a red peony fan with a blue Tang ruqun creates more visual interest than a blue fan with a blue dress.</li>
        <li><strong>Occasion:</strong> Reserve heavily painted, ornate fans for photoshoots and formal events. A simple bamboo folding fan or plain silk tuanshan is more appropriate for casual meetups and daily wear.</li>
        <li><strong>Care:</strong> Never fold a damp paper fan; let it air-dry fully open. Store folding fans loosely closed (not tightly compressed) to preserve the paper. Silk tuanshan should be stored flat or hanging, away from direct sunlight to prevent fading.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Experience Fan Culture and Shopping in China</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">China’s fan-making tradition is concentrated in a few historic cities where you can visit artisan workshops and buy directly from the source.</p>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Hangzhou – Wangxingji Fan Shop & Hefang Street</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> Wangxingji, established 1875, is China’s most prestigious fan maker. Their flagship on Hefang Street is part shop, part museum – watch artisans hand-paint silk fan surfaces and carve sandalwood ribs. Hefang Street has a dozen other fan shops plus tea houses and snack vendors along a pedestrian-only historic street.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> Fly into Hangzhou Xiaoshan Airport or high-speed train from Shanghai (1 hour, 75-115 RMB). Metro Line 1 to Ding’an Road, 10-minute walk to Hefang Street. West Lake is a 15-minute walk south.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> Hefang Street area hotels (300-600 RMB/night). West Lake scenic hotels (500-2,000 RMB/night). Budget: GreenTree Inn near Metro stations (200-350 RMB/night).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Dongpo rou (braised pork belly, 50-80 RMB), longjing xiaren (shrimp with Dragon Well tea, 60-100 RMB). Zhiweiguan near Hefang Street serves classic Hangzhou cuisine (60-120 RMB/person).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Shopping tips:</strong> Wangxingji sandalwood folding fans start at 200 RMB (to 2,000+). Smaller shops on Hefang Street have fans from 50-200 RMB. Open and close any folding fan gently to check the action before buying.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Suzhou – Tanmuzhe & Pingjiang Road</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> Suzhou’s round silk fans (tuanshan) are the finest in China. Tanmuzhe on Pingjiang Road is run by a recognized fan artisan who paints each surface by hand. Custom commissions (allow 3-7 days) are available. The Suzhou Museum (designed by I.M. Pei, free entry) has a collection of historical fans.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> High-speed rail from Shanghai (25 min, 40-80 RMB). Metro Line 1 to Lindun Road, walk or rickshaw (20-30 RMB) to Pingjiang Road.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> Pingjiang Road courtyard guesthouses (350-700 RMB/night). Pan Pacific Suzhou (800-1,500 RMB/night) in a historic residence.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Xie ke huang (crab-shell pastry, 5-10 RMB each), tangyuan (glutinous rice balls, 15-25 RMB). Songhelou on Guanqian Street for refined Suzhou cuisine (80-200 RMB/person).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Shopping tips:</strong> Custom tuanshan at Tanmuzhe: 300-800 RMB. Bring a reference image for custom designs. Allow 3-7 days for custom work.</p>
      </div>
